65 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265665 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265665 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265665 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265665 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265665 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186265027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265665 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb7af93c-6457-57a3-b69c-ee3def8e915e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265757 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265757 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265757 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,3.03).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265757 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46fcafee-3ead-50fb-9300-00e4d05cfb60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265757 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,3)+∇f(1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈4+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,3) & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,3) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.04,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04$ and $y=3.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,3.03) & ≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =4+5(0.04)+1(0.03) \\\\  & =4+0.2+0.03 \\\\  & =4.23\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265762 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265762 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265762 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73178492-8508-56c4-9569-cdff37299fa0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265762 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,-1)+∇f(1,0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,0,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,-1),{f}_{y}(1,0,-1),{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02,$ $y=0.1,$ and $z=-0.96$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.1,-0.96) & ≈1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.1,-0.96+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =1-1(0.02)-1(0.1)+0(0.04) \\\\  & =1-0.02-0.1+0 \\\\  & =0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265766 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265766 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265766 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d09800e-3081-5040-b777-174413c7401e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265766 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,-1)+∇f(0,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-1,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,-1),{f}_{y}(0,1,-1),{f}_{z}(0,1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,1,-1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,-1) & =(-1){e}^{0}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,1,-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,1,-1) & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.01,y=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.01,1.01,-0.99) & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2-1(0.01)-1(0.01)+1(0.01) \\\\  & ≈-2-0.01-0.01+0.01 \\\\  & ≈-2.01\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265772 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f8175f3-53d3-588b-a7a3-5b2abc348ee7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265772 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1,1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,-1,1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =y\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,-1,1),{f}_{y}(0,-1,1),{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-(x)+(y+1)-(z-1) \\\\  & ≈1-x+y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265773 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265773 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265773 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265773 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265773 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5440338c-d1fc-538a-8b78-57b3659475c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265773 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1,-1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+(x-1)-(y+1)-(z+1) \\\\  & ≈-2+x-y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321277 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{y-1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321277 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321277 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1065024a-3269-5b4e-9b97-80e6b4a8d214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321667 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1+x+y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "41b1ed5b-06f3-573c-a70d-e7a1c46121ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322347 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{y-1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322347 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322347 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "967e34ca-205a-50ff-8865-ca5c930362a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322386 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322386 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322386 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d7d0daf-52c8-5485-a882-46b1726df241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322609 #edb/ref 1080 "f(x,y)≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322609 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322609 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc8b4b62-6cbb-5056-9cda-092b1beb632f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323361 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323361 #edb/ref 1075 #